IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `imported-domain`

§ `An exported decision built entirely out of imported declarations`

-- REGRESSION EXHIBIT. Until 2026-09-21 the lowering scanned only the module it
-- was handed, so a `DECLARE` in an imported file was invisible to it even
-- though the typechecker had resolved the name perfectly well. The symptom did
-- not say "imported": it said
--
--     in `assess`: type `Assessment` is outside the v1 Catala fragment (§6)
--
-- which reads as a language limit rather than a missing scan, and there was no
-- way to act on it. Three things are pinned here at once:
--
--   1. an imported RECORD as a `GIVETH` and as a construction site;
--   2. an imported ENUMERATION, reached only through that record's field —
--      so it is emitted because something else that is emitted needs it, not
--      because anything names it here;
--   3. an imported HELPER, which R1 lowers to a private toplevel in this
--      module exactly as it would a local one. (The alternative — refusing an
--      imported helper and telling the author to inline it — was considered
--      and declined: a helper is a helper wherever it was written.)
--
-- What is NOT emitted matters as much. This file imports the prelude, whose
-- own `DECLARE`s are now equally visible to the scan; an imported declaration
-- reaches the artifact only when the emitted code reaches it, so the emitted
-- module below carries `Assessment` and `Band` and nothing of the stdlib.

@export The assessment for an income: the taxable amount, and the band it falls in.
GIVEN income IS A NUMBER
GIVETH AN Assessment
assess income MEANS
    Assessment WITH
        `taxable amount` IS income TIMES 0.9
        band             IS `band for` income

@export Whether an income is assessed in the upper band.
GIVEN income IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`in the upper band` income MEANS
    CONSIDER (assess income)'s band
    WHEN Upper THEN TRUE
    WHEN Lower THEN FALSE

#EVAL assess 2000

#EVAL `in the upper band` 2000

#EVAL `in the upper band` 500
